# ── Dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/versions

# ── Build output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dist/ is generated, so it stays out of git: `npm run build` rebuilds it from
# minichart.js, and `prepublishOnly` runs that build before every publish, so
# the tarball on npm always carries a freshly built bundle. unpkg and jsDelivr
# serve the npm tarball, not this repo — nothing external reads dist/ from here.
#
# Consequence, deliberately accepted: `npm run build:check` compares dist/ on
# disk against a fresh build, so in a clean checkout it reports "out of date"
# until something builds. `npm test` builds first (test:dist), so the suite is
# unaffected; only build:check as a standalone CI staleness gate loses meaning.
# Committing dist/ again is the one-line reversal if you want that gate back.
dist/

# Note: docs/minichart.min.js is the site's own copy, written by `site:sync`,
# and stays committed — GitHub Pages serves docs/ straight from the repo, so
# ignoring it would 404 on minichart.org. Do not widen the rule above to
# *.min.js.

# npm pack / publish artefacts
*.tgz
.npm/

# A second lockfile from another package manager silently changes what CI
# resolves. This project is npm-only — package-lock.json is the one that counts.
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml
bun.lockb

# ── Local scratch and debugging ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ad-hoc probes and manual Playwright stands used while chasing a specific bug.
# Not part of the test suite: `npm test` covers the same ground reproducibly.
_verify/
_*.cjs
_*.mjs

# Comparison / full-page captures taken while checking the site by eye.
cmp-*.png
full-page.png

# Unreferenced: no page embeds it, and a 1 MB capture of an animation the live
# demo already runs is not worth versioning.
docs/demo-streaming.gif

# Internal design notes. Nothing links to them, and they are not needed to
# build, test, publish or use the library — README.md and docs/docs.html are the
# documentation that ships. Kept locally, out of the repo.
ARCHITECTURE.md

# ── Every dot-directory, anywhere in the tree ────────────────────────────────
# Deny-by-default rather than chasing names. AI assistants, editors, linters and
# task runners each drop their own dot-directory — .claude/, .cursor/, .aider*/,
# .vscode/, .idea/, .venv/, .turbo/, .parcel-cache/ — and a per-tool list is
# always one tool behind. Some of them hold conversation logs, prompts, indexed
# copies of the source, or cached credentials; none of it belongs in a public
# repository. Nothing this project needs to build, test or publish lives in a
# dot-directory, so blanket-ignoring them costs nothing.
#
# Dot-FILES are untouched by this: `.*/` matches directories only, so
# .gitignore itself stays tracked. Secrets in dot-files are handled further
# down (.env, .npmrc, keys).
.*/

# The one exception. GitHub reads workflows only from a committed .github/, so
# an ignored one means CI silently never runs. Re-included as a directory —
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so the
# negation has to name the directory, exactly as it does here.
!.github/

# Explicit anyway, for anyone reading this file rather than testing it: these
# are the ones already seen in this tree. All are covered by `.*/` above.
.playwright-mcp/
.playwright/
.nyc_output/
.claude/
.cursor/
.aider*

# Tool output that is NOT a dot-directory, so it needs its own rules.
playwright-report/
test-results/
blob-report/
coverage/
*.lcov

# Host-specific screenshots and captures anywhere in the tree.
*.png.orig
screenshot*.png
Screenshot*.png

